<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alfred Dreyfus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1859">1859</a>–<a href="/years/1935">1935</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alfred Dreyfus was a French artillery officer from a Jewish Alsatian family. In 1894 he was falsely convicted of passing military secrets to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and sent to the penal colony on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devils-island">Devil's Island</a> in French Guiana. The campaign to clear him, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dreyfus-affair">Dreyfus Affair</a>, divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>; he was pardoned in 1899, fully exonerated in 1906 and returned to the army, serving in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alfred-dreyfus.jpg" alt="Alfred Dreyfus" width="320" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aron Gerschel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alfred_Dreyfus_(1859-1935)_-_photo_originale.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Alfred Dreyfus was born in Mulhouse, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace">Alsace</a>, on 9 October 1859, the youngest of nine children of Raphaël and Jeannette Dreyfus. His family belonged to a long established Alsatian Jewish family, and his father became a prosperous industrialist who started a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> mill. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franco-prussian-war">Franco-Prussian War</a> of 1870 and the German annexation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alsace-lorraine">Alsace-Lorraine</a>, the family moved to Basel and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Seeing his family uprooted by the war prompted him to choose a military career.</p><p>He entered the École Polytechnique in October 1877 and was commissioned a sub-lieutenant in 1880. He became a captain in 1889, married Lucie Eugénie Hadamard in 1891, and graduated ninth in his class from the École Supérieure de Guerre, afterwards joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-general-staff">General Staff</a> as its only Jewish officer.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>A torn-up note, the bordereau, was found in a wastebasket at the German Embassy and passed to French intelligence. It showed that information on artillery was reaching the German military attaché Maximilian von Schwartzkoppen. Suspicion fell on Dreyfus, who was arrested for treason on 15 October 1894. A closed court martial convicted him on 22 December 1894 and sentenced him to life imprisonment on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devils-island">Devil's Island</a>. On 5 January 1895 he was formally degraded in the courtyard of the École Militaire, crying out: I swear that I am innocent.</p><p>In August 1896 Lieutenant Colonel Georges Picquart found evidence that the traitor was Major Ferdinand Walsin Esterhazy, and was transferred to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Esterhazy was acquitted by a secret court martial. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emile-zola">Émile Zola</a>'s J'Accuse...! of 1898 brought new momentum to the campaign. A second trial in 1899 again found Dreyfus guilty, but President Émile Loubet pardoned him that year. A military commission exonerated him on 12 July 1906, and he was reinstated as a major.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Dreyfus returned as a reserve artillery major when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> began, was promoted to lieutenant colonel, and served mostly behind the lines, though in 1917 he was at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/verdun">Verdun</a> and the Chemin des Dames. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> on 12 July 1935, aged 75, and was buried in the Montparnasse Cemetery. A statue of Dreyfus holding his broken sword stands on Boulevard Raspail, and his family gave over three thousand documents to the Museum of Jewish Art and History in Paris.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alfred Dreyfus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A French military court convicted the Jewish officer <b>Alfred Dreyfus</b> of passing secrets to Germany and sentenced him to deportation for life.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-22">22 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Zola's open letter in L'Aurore accused the French army of convicting the Jewish officer <b>Alfred Dreyfus</b> wrongly and covering up the truth.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-13">13 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Captain <b>Alfred Dreyfus</b>, wrongly convicted of treason in December 1894, was degraded at the Military School in Paris: his badges were torn off and his sword broken.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-5">5 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>From 1852 to 1953 the colony held a penal colony, the bagne, including the Îles du Salut, where <b>Alfred Dreyfus</b> was held on Devil's Island.</blockquote><a href="/state/french-guiana">French Guiana</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Mulhouse, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.75,7.34&amp;year=1908" aria-label="Mulhouse on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4721 491 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Alfred Dreyfus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5171 761h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5171 761h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.75,7.34&amp;year=1908">What was here in 1908? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We are not living in a century when the light can be hidden. It must be that the whole truth will be known, that my voice will be heard throughout the length and breadth of our dear France—just as my accusation has been heard.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to his wife Lucie from prison, December 1894 (Lettres d'un innocent, 1898) &#183; tr. L. G. Moreau, 1899 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/64720/pg64720-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;To be innocent, to have lived a life without a stain, and to be condemned for the most monstrous crime that a soldier can commit! What could be more terrible? It seems to me at times that I am the victim of an awful nightmare.&#8221;</p><footer>Letter to his wife Lucie from prison, December 1894 (Lettres d'un innocent, 1898) &#183; tr. L. G. Moreau, 1899 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/64720/pg64720-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
